  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 206 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThe High circulation will drift southward on Wednesday, drawing \nmoisture southward into central NM. This will expand the coverage of \nshowers and storms into western NM, which will continue to favor the \nhigh terrain. Isolated, erratic wind gusts of 30 to 40 mph will be \nthe main hazard with this activity. DCAPE increasing to around 750 \nJ/kg late Wednesday afternoon suggests the potential for isolated \ngusts upwards of 50 mph where a few stronger updrafts could develop.\nRidging will gradually deamplify over the Intermountain West late \nweek, placing modest zonal flow over New Mexico. Temps will trend \ndown slightly as a result, with temps hovering right around seasonal \nnormals. \n\nMore active weather looks to return to the desert southwest this \nweekend into the early part of next week. Troughing will develop \nover the West Coast of the US, drawing a plume of Pacific moisture \nnorthward into the Four Corners region. Around a quarter of models \n(mostly European ensemble members) attempt to bring moisture from \nnow Hurricane Rachel into the desert southwest, but the majority \n(75%) keep Rachel's circulation south and only shear mid and high \nlevel moisture northward. The current forecast has rain chances \nfavoring the western third of New Mexico early to mid-week, with \norographic lift bringing higher precip totals to the mountains. Most \nlikely rainfall amounts for the western high terrain are in the 0.1-\n0.5\" range, but these will likely trend higher or lower in the \ncoming days.",
